.It Xo Ic tui
.Xc
Menu-driven terminal manager over the same surface this page documents,
laid out like
.Em Midnight Commander :
a bar of menus along the top, panels that fill the window, and a
function-key legend along the bottom.
The panels are
.Em overview ,
.Em patches ,
.Em curated catalog ,
.Em omlx-dev keg
and
.Em session log ;
the bar holds
.Em Go ,
.Em Patches ,
.Em Catalog ,
.Em Keg ,
.Em Services ,
.Em View
and
.Em Help .
.Ic F9
(or
.Ic Alt
plus the menu letter) opens a menu, the arrows walk it,
.Ic Enter
runs the highlighted command and
.Ic Esc
closes it.
A command that cannot run right now stays visible and greyed with the
reason \(em a disabled command that explains itself is easier to learn
than one that is hidden.
Menu commands act on the row their panel has selected, the same way
panel commands do.
.Pp
In a panel the arrow keys (or
.Ic j Ns / Ns Ic k )
move,
.Ic Enter
opens that row's action menu,
.Ic Tab
and
.Ic \&left / right
switch panels,
.Ic F3
toggles the detail pane,
.Ic F5
re-reads live state,
.Ic T
cycles the colour theme,
.Ic F1
lists the keys,
.Ic F10
or
.Ic q
quits.
The number keys
.Ic 1 Ns - Ns Ic 5
jump straight to a panel and every action keeps its letter shortcut;
the bar is an additional path, never a worse one.
A mouse click selects a row, opens a menu and runs a menu item.
.Pp
The TUI implements no policy of its own.
Every action calls the function the dashboard route calls, or runs the CLI
verb named on screen, so
.Ic tui
and a shell can never disagree about what a button does.
Nothing writes until you confirm: a state change asks
.Ic y Ns / Ns Ic N ,
and anything that touches tree bytes, a keg, or a running service asks you to
type
.Ic YES
on its own line \(em
.Ic q
cancels an armed question instead of answering it.
.Pp
Colour themes:
.Em default
(terminal's own colours),
.Em p(doom)
(the SHODAN dashboard skin in the terminal \(em void black, laser crimson,
ember orange),
.Em phosphor
(single-hue CRT) and
.Em mono
(no colour, for screenshots and broken
.Ev TERM ) .
A 256-colour terminal that allows palette writes gets the exact RGB values;
otherwise the nearest available colour is used and the status line says which,
because a silently mis-coloured warning is worse than an honest fallback.
A degraded palette is reported under the panels, never hidden.
The choice is remembered in
.Pa tui.json .
.Pp
Requires a terminal on both stdin and stdout and a usable
